.TH TREE 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
tree \- list directories as a tree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tree
.RB [ \-a ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-L
.IR level ]
.RB [ \-\-noreport ]
.RI [ dir ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tree
prints each
.I dir
(the working directory when none is named), then its contents as a tree,
drawn with lines, descending into every directory. A symbolic link is
shown as
.I name
.B \->
.I target
and is not followed. Names starting with a dot are left out.
.PP
At the end it reports how many directories and files it showed, for all
the dirs together.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Shows names starting with a dot too.
.TP
.B \-d
Shows directories only; the report then counts only directories.
.TP
.BI \-L " level"
Descends at most
.I level
levels (1: only the directory's own entries). It must be greater than 0.
.TP
.B \-\-noreport
Leaves the report out.
.PP
.B \-a
and
.B \-d
can be joined
.RB ( \-ad ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 for an invalid option or level; 2 if a
.I dir
isn't a directory (it is shown as
.IB dir " [error opening dir]"
and the others are still listed).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
tree
tree \-L 1 /
tree \-d /lib
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ls (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR du (1)
